</Tags>
<Routines>
<Routine Name="fault_program" Type="RLL"/>
<Routine Name="MainRoutine" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The ControlLogix controller does not use a status file like the PLC5 and SLC500 programmable controllers.  To get some status information from the ControlLogix controller and it's modules the GSV (Get System Value) and SSV (Set System Value) instructions are used.  Another difference between the ControlLogix controller and PLC5's is that in the PLC5 the status file was updated automatically in the housekeeping.  With the ControlLogix controller this automatic update in housekeeping is not done.  This is to cut down on housekeeping time.  So, keep in mind that to get this status information ladder logic is needed to be created and programmed for when and how the information is needed. 

This program was devised to show what information from the PLC5 status file can used in the ControlLogix controller.  It shows examples on how to get or set the information for a ControlLogix controller.  It can be used as examples.  It is set up to allow a copy/paste into another file.  Unfortunately,  MSG instructions may need to be re-configured.  All set up information for the MSG instructions are explained in their appropriate rung comments.  ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:0
Shows ControlLogix version of the Arithmetic Flags]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S0_Arithmetic_Flags,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:1
Shows ControlLogix version of the Processor Status and Flags.
Included are S:1/01Processor in run mode
S:1/02 Processor in test mode
S:1/03 Processor in program mode
S:1/06 Processor has test edits enabled
S:1/07 Mode select switch in Remote position 
S:1/08 Forces enabled
S:1/09 Forces present
S:1/14 Last scan of ladder
S:1/15 Processor running first program scan

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:  
S:1/00 Ram Checksum
S:1/04 Processor uploading to memory module 
S:1/05 Processor in download mode
S:1/10 Processor succesfully uploaded to memory module
S:1/11 Performin online programming
S:1/12 Processor in Debug Mode
S:1/13 User program checksum calculated]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S1_Processor_Status,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:2 DH+ Switch Setting Inforamation
S:3 - S:6 Active Node Table
Included are S:2/00 - S:2/05 DH+ Staition Number
S:2/06 DH+ Baud Rate

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits: 
S:2/07 & S:2/08 Not Defined
S:2/09 Last State
S:2/11 & S:2/12  I/O Chassis Addressing
S:2/13 & S:2/14  Memory Module Transfer
S:2/15 Processor Memory Protection

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S2_S6_DHP,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:8 Last Program Scan
S:9 Maximum Program Scan

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:
S:7/00-07 Racks 00 - 07 Faulted bits
S:7/08-15 Block Transfer queue to Racks 00 - 07 is full.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S8_S9_Prog_scan_times,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:10 Minor Fault (word 1)
S:17 Minor Fault (word 2)
Included for S:10 are
S:10/00 Battery is low
S:10/02 STI (Periodic Task) interrupt program overlap
S:10/04 Arithmetic overflow

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:
S:10/01 DH+ Active node table has changed
S:10/03 Memory module transferred at power up
S:10/04 data table size changed during program
S:10/05 Invalid I/O Status File
S:10/07 No more command blocks for block transfers
S:10/08 Not enough memory to upload in memory module
S:10/09 No MCP is configured to run
S:10/10 MCP not allowed
S:10/11 PII word number not in local rack
S:10/12  PII Overlap
S:10/13 No command blocks exist to get PII
S:10/15 SFC action overlap
S:13 File Number where fault occurred
S:14 Rung Number where fault occurred

Included for S:17 is S:17/05 No modem on serial port
S:17/09 Duplicate Node Addr

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:
S:17/00 - S:17/04 Queue Full Block Transfers
S:17/06 Remote I/0 rack in local rack table
S:17/07 Firmware revision for channel pairs does not match processor firmware revision
S:17/08 ASCII instruction error
S:17/10 DF1 master poll list error
S:17/11 & S:17/12 Protected processor violations
S:17/13 - S:17/15 ControlNet minor faults
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S10_and_S17_Minor_Faults,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:11 Major Fault Word
S:12 Major Fault Codes
S:13 Program file where fault occured
S:14 Rung number where fault occured

Included for S:11 are S:11/05 Start up protection fault
S:11/08 Watchdog faulted
S:11/14 Fault routine does not exist

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:
S:11/0 Corrupted program file
S:11/1 Corrupted address in ladder program
S:11/2 Program error
S:11/3 SFC Fault
S:11/4 Duplicate LBL's
S:11/6 Peripheral device fault
S:11/7 User generated fault
S:11/9 System Configured wrong
S:11/10 Recoverable hardware error
S:11/11 MCP does not exist
S:11/12 PII file does not exist
S:11/13 STI file does not exist
S:11/15 Faulted program file does not contain ladder logic


]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S11_S14_Major_Faults,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S18 - S23 Processor Date & Time

Included are S:18 Processor Clock year
S:19 Processor Clock Month
S:20 Processor Clock Day
S:21 Processor Clock Hour
S:22 Processor Clock Minute
S:23 Processor Clock Second]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S18_S23_Processor_Clock,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:24 Indexed Addressing offset - Not Available
S:25 Reserved
S:26 User Control Bits

None of the S:26 User Control Bits are available]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S24_S26_Control_Bits,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:27, S:33, S:35 Rack Control Bits are not available
S:28 Watchdog Setpoint
S:29 Fault Routine File
S:30 STI (Periodic Task) Setpoint
S:31 STI File Number
S:32 & S:34 Global Status Bits are not available]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S27_S35_DHP_STI_Fault_Stuff,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:36 - S:52 are PII information which is not available
S:55 & S:56 are PII information which is not available
S:53 STI (Periodic Task) time
S:54 STI (Periodic Task) maximum scan time
S:57 User program checksum is not available
S:58 Reserved is not applicable
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S36_S58_PII_STI_Stuff,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:59 - S:62 Is Extended Local I/O information which is not applicable
S:64 Protected processor information is not applicable
S:65 Remote Block transfer information for channel 2A/2B is not applicable
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S59_S65_LIO_RIO_Stuff,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="12" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:66 Reserved is not applicable
S:77 Communication Time Slice]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S66_S77_TimeSlice,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="13" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:78 - S:127 MCP information is not applicable]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(S78_S127_MCP_Stuff,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S0_Arithmetic_Flags" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:0/2 Arithmetic zero bit
Use the already defined s:z.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(s:z)OTE(_Status_zero);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:0/1 & S:10/14 Arithmetic Overflow bit
Use the already defined s:v.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(s:v)OTE(_Status_overflow);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:0/3 Arithmetic negative sign bit.
Use the already defined s:n.
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(s:n)OTE(_Status_sign);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:0/0 Arithmetic carry bit.
Use the already defined s:c.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(s:c)OTE(_Status_carry);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S10_and_S17_Minor_Faults" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:10 Minor Fault (word 1)
S:17 Minor Fault (word 2)
Included for S:10 are
S:10/00 Battery is low
S:10/02 STI (Periodic Task) interrupt program overlap
S:10/04 Arithmetic overflow

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:
S:10/01 DH+ Active node table has changed
S:10/03 Memory module transferred at power up
S:10/04 data table size changed during program
S:10/05 Invalid I/O Status File
S:10/07 No more command blocks for block transfers
S:10/08 Not enough memory to upload in memory module
S:10/09 No MCP is configured to run
S:10/10 MCP not allowed
S:10/11 PII word number not in local rack
S:10/12  PII Overlap
S:10/13 No command blocks exist to get PII
S:10/15 SFC action overlap
S:13 File Number where fault occurred
S:14 Rung Number where fault occurred

Included for S:17 is S:17/05 No modem on serial port
S:17/09 Duplicate Node Addr

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:
S:17/00 - S:17/04 Queue Full Block Transfers
S:17/06 Remote I/O rack Adapter size overlap
S:17/07 Firmware revision for channel pairs does not match processor firmware revision
S:17/08 ASCII instruction error
S:17/10 DF1 master poll list error
S:17/11 & S:17/12 Protected processor violations
S:17/13 - S:17/15 ControlNet minor faults
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:17/09 Duplicate Node Address
Use a CIP Generic MSG instruction to get information from the 1756-DHRIO module.   Set the values to the following:  Service Code 'e', Class Name 'a5', Instance Name '1', &  Attribute Name '7'.  The Source section is left blank and the number of elements is zero.   For this information a user defined data type called _Status_counters was created.  This attribute gets information for a number of diagnostic counters.  You can get a count on the number of duplicate nodes by using the user defined address of _Status_counters.duplicate_node.  The Path should point to the 1756-DHRIO module.  In this example we have set up the DHRIO module in the I/O Configuarion.  It is named dhp and this is address which is named in the path.  This CIP Generic MSG can also be used for channel B by changing the Object ID to 2.
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(_Status_dupl_node.EN)MSG(_Status_dupl_node);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:10/0 (battery low) , S:10/2 (Periodic Task overlap/not completed), S:10/14 (arithmetic overflow), & S:17/5 (serial port error) bits.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the FaultLog object and MinorFaultBits Attribute to get some minor fault information.  Bits 12 - 15 are used for this information.  The next 4 rungs show how to use the GSV ControllerDevice object to read the proceesor mode information.  See the GSV/SSV ControllerDevice Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(FAULTLOG,,MinorFaultBits,_Status_minorfaults);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:10/0 Battery Low Minor Fault
Use with the GSV FaultLog Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_minorfaults.10)OTE(_Status_battery_low);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:10/2 STI (Periodic Task) Overlap/NotCompleted Minor Fault
Use with the GSV FaultLog Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_minorfaults.6)OTE(_Status_Periodic_task);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:17/05 No Modem on Serial port (Serial Port or RS232 Error)  Minor Fault
Use with the GSV FaultLog Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_minorfaults.9)OTE(_Status_rS232_error);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:0/1 & S:10/14 Arithmetic Overflow bit
Use the already defined s:v.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(s:v)OTE(_Status_overflow);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S11_S14_Major_Faults" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:11 Major Fault Word
S:12 Major Fault Codes
S:13 Program file where fault occured
S:14 Rung number where fault occured

Included for S:11 are S:11/05 Start up protection fault
S:11/08 Watchdog faulted
S:11/14 Fault routine does not exist
ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:
S:11/0 Corrupted program file
S:11/1 Corrupted address in ladder program
S:11/2 Program error
S:11/3 SFC Fault
S:11/4 Duplicate LBL's
S:11/6 Peripheral device fault
S:11/7 User generated fault
S:11/9 System Configured wrong
S:11/10 Recoverable hardware error
S:11/11 MCP does not exist
S:11/12 PII file does not exist
S:11/13 STI file does not exist
S:11/15 Faulted program file does not contain ladder logic
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:11/08 (watchdog fault) , S:11/5 (Start up protection fault), & S:11/14 (fault routine does not exist) bits.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value)  command with the FaultLog object and MajorFaultBits Attribute to get some minor fault information.  The next 3 rungs show how to use the GSV FaultLog object to read the major fault information.  See the GSV/SSV FaultLog Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(FAULTLOG,,MajorFaultBits,_Status_majorfaults);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:11/8 Watch Dog Timeout Major Fault
Use with the GSV FaultLog Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_majorfaults.6)OTE(_Status_Watchdog_faulted);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:11/5 Power Loss Major Fault
Use with the GSV FaultLog Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_majorfaults.1)OTE(_Status_Power_loss);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:11/14 Fault Routine does not exist Major Fault
Use with the GSV FaultLog Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_majorfaults.5)OTE(_Status_Fault_handler);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:12 Major Fault Codes.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value)  command with the Program object and MajorFaultRecord Attribute to get the Major Fault Type, Code, & Information.  See the GSV/SSV Program Object for specific details. The Major Fault Record can use either a GSV to read the value or an SSV to write the value to the processor.    A user defined data type called _Status_BigFaults_type was created for the destination.  The tag results uses the _Status_BigFaults_type user defined data type.  Use the THIS function in the Instance Name to select the current program which is executing.
Even though the Major Fault is cleared the fault code is retained in the processor.  But the fault code can be overwritten when the controller is placed into RUN mode. A routine should be added to save this value.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(PROGRAM,THIS,MAJORFAULTRECORD,_Status_results.TimeLow);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Use the GSV MajorFault Record above to get the specific Major fault information get the information from the results tag.  This rung shows how to get the Major Fault Type, Major fault code, and the major Fault Record Information.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(_Status_results.Type,_Status_Fault_type) ,MOV(_Status_results.Code,_Status_Fault_code) ,MOV(_Status_results.Info[0],_Status_Fault_info) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S18_S23_Processor_Clock" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S18 - S23 Processor Date & Time

Included are S:18 Processor Clock year
S:19 Processor Clock Month
S:20 Processor Clock Day
S:21 Processor Clock Hour
S:22 Processor Clock Minute
S:23 Processor Clock Second]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Use the GSV Wallclocktime object, DateTime attribute to get the data from the processor.  Use a MOV instruction with the appropriate array from the GSV to move the data into a location.  
PLC5's S:18 - 23 Processor Date & Time.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the WallClockTime object and DataTime Attribute to get the Processor Data & Time information.   A tag with an array of 7 DINT types will  need to be created for the destination. See the GSV/SSV WallClockTime Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(WALLCLOCKTIME,,DateTime,_Status_time[0]);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:18 - S:20 Processor Date
Use the GSV Wallclocktime object above to get the Processor date information.  Use the MOV instruction with the appropriate array from the GSV to move the data into a location. Array[0] is year, Array[1] is month, and Array[2] is day.  ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(_Status_time[0],_Status_Year) ,MOV(_Status_time[1],_Status_Month) ,MOV(_Status_time[2],_Status_Day) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:21 - S:23 Processor Time
Use the GSV Wallclocktime object above to get the Processor time information.  Use the MOV instruction with the appropriate array from the GSV to move the data into a location. Array[3] is hour, Array[4] is minute, and Array[5] is seconds.  There is also an Array[6] for microseconds.  ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(_Status_time[3],_Status_Hour) ,MOV(_Status_time[4],_Status_Minute) ,MOV(_Status_time[5],_Status_Seconds) ,MOV(_Status_time[6],_Status_Microsec) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S1_Processor_Status" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:1
Shows ControlLogix version of the Processor Status and Flags.
Included are S:1/01Processor in run mode
S:1/02 Processor in test mode
S:1/03 Processor in program mode
S:1/06 Processor has test edits enabled
S:1/07 Mode select switch in Remote position 
S:1/08 Forces enabled
S:1/09 Forces present
S:1/14 Last scan of ladder
S:1/15 Processor running first program scan

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits:  
S:1/00 Ram Checksum
S:1/04 Processor uploading to memory module 
S:1/05 Processor in download mode
S:1/10 Processor succesfully uploaded to memory module
S:1/11 Performin online programming
S:1/12 Processor in Debug Mode
S:1/13 User program checksum calculated]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/0 (run mode) , S:1/2 (test mode), S:1/3 (program mode), & S:1/7 (remote mode) bits.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the ControllerDevice object and Status Attribute to get processor mode information.  Bits 12 - 15 are used for this information.  The next 4 rungs show how to use the GSV ControllerDevice object to read the proceesor mode information.  See the GSV/SSV ControllerDevice Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(CONTROLLERDEVICE,,Status,_Status_mode);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/01 Run Mode
Use with the GSV/SSV ControllerDevice Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(_Status_mode.13)XIC(_Status_mode.12)OTE(_Status_key_run);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/03 Program Mode
Use with the GSV/SSV ControllerDevice Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_mode.13)XIO(_Status_mode.12)OTE(_Status_key_prog);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/07 Remote Mode
Use with the GSV/SSV ControllerDevice Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_mode.13)XIC(_Status_mode.12)OTE(_Status_key_remote);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/02 Test Mode
Use with the GSV/SSV ControllerDevice Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_mode.15)XIO(_Status_mode.14)OTE(_Status_test_mode);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/6 test edits enabled bit.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the Program object and DisableFlag Attribute to get test edits enabled information.  Use the THIS function in the Instance Name to select the current program which is executing.   A tag with a SINT type will need to be created for the destination. If the value is 0 than edits are enabled if the value is 1 than edits are disabled.  See the GSV/SSV Program Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(PROGRAM,THIS,DISABLEFLAG,_Status_test_edits);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/06  Test Edits Enabled
Use with the GSV/SSV Program Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(_Status_test_edits,0)OTE(_Status_edits_enabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/06  Test Edits Enabled
Use with the GSV/SSV Program Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(_Status_test_edits,1)OTE(_Status_edits_disabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/8 (forces enabled)  & S:1/9 (forces present) bits.   
Uses only  the GSV (Get System Value) command with the Module object and ForceStatus Attribute to get the forcing information.  A tag with a INT type will need to be created for the destination. Bits 0  will be analyzed to determine whether forces are installed and bit 1 will determine whether or not forces are enabled.  See the GSV/SSV Module Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(MODULE,,ForceStatus,_Status_forces);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/09  Forces Present
Use with the GSV Module Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_forces.0)OTE(_Status_forces_present);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/09  Forces Enabled
Use with the GSV Module Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(_Status_forces.1)OTE(_Status_forces_enabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="12" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/14 Last Scan of Ladder.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) command with the Task object and LastScanTime Attribute to get the value of the last scan time of a particular task.  The Instance Name can select a particular task or use the THIS name to select the current executing task.  A tag with a DINT type will need to be created for the destination.  See the GSV/SSV Task Object for specific details. ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(TASK,SecondTask,LASTSCANTIME,_Status_taskscan_time);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="13" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/14  Last Scan Time of Task
Use with the GSV Task Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(_Status_taskscan_time,_Status_Task_Scantime);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="14" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:1/15 First Scan bit
Use the already defined s:fs.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(s:fs)OTE(_Status_first_scan);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S24_S26_Control_Bits" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:24 Indexed Addressing offset - Not Available
S:25 Reserved
S:26 User Control Bits

None of the S:26 User Control Bits are available]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S27_S35_DHP_STI_Fault_Stuff" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:27, S:33, S:35 Rack Control Bits are not available
S:28 Watchdog Setpoint
S:29 Fault Routine File
S:30 STI (Periodic Task) Setpoint
S:31 STI File Number
S:32 & S:34 Global Status Bits are not available]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:28 Watchdog Setpoint.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the Task object and WatchDog Attribute to get or modify the setpoint value of the watchdog  time of a particular task.  The CIP Object Name can select a particular task or use the THIS name to select the whole program.  A tag with a DINT type will need to be created for the destination.  See the GSV/SSV Task Object for specific details. ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(TASK,SecondTask,WATCHDOG,_Status_Task_watchdog);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:30 STI (Periodic Task) Setpoint.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) command with the Task object and Rate Attribute to get or modify the setpoint value of a particular task.  The Instance Name can select a particular task or use the THIS name to select the current executing task.  A tag with a DINT type will need to be created for the destination.  See the GSV/SSV Task Object for specific details. ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(TASK,SecondTask,RATE,_Status_Task_rate);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S2_S6_DHP" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:2 DH+ Switch Setting Inforamation
S:3 - S:6 Active Node Table
Included are S:2/00 - S:2/05 DH+ Staition Number
S:2/06 DH+ Baud Rate

ControlLogix either does not have the functionality or need for the following bits: 
S:2/07 & S:2/08 Not Defined
S:2/09 Last State
S:2/11 & S:2/12  I/O Chassis Addressing
S:2/13 & S:2/14  Memory Module Transfer
S:2/15 Processor Memory Protection

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:2/00 - S:2/05  Channel 1A DH+ Station Number.
Use a CIP Generic MSG instruction to get information from the 1756-DHRIO module.   Set the values to the following:  Service Code 'e', Class Name 'a5', Instance Name '1', & Attribute name '1'.  The Source section is left blank and the number of elements is zero.  A destination tag of the SINT type needs to be created.  In this example it is called _Status_Node.  The Path should point to the 1756-DHRIO module.  In this example we have set up the DHRIO module in the I/O Configuarion.  It is named dhp and this is address which is named in the path.  This CIP Generic MSG can also be used for channel B by changine the Object ID to 2.
The tag address will show the node of the DH+ node of the selected 1756-DHRIO module.  The value will be displayed in decimal format.  It will need to be converted to Octal to reflect the address displayed on the module.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(_Status_node_addr.EN)MSG(_Status_node_addr);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:2/06 Channel 1A DH+ Baud Rate
Use a CIP Generic MSG instruction to get information from the 1756-DHRIO module.   Set the values to the following:  Service Code 'e', Class Name 'a5', Instance Name '1', & Attribute Name '3'.  The Source section is left blank and the number of elements is zero.  A destination tag of the SINT type needs to be created.  In this example it is called _Status_ch1_baud.   The Path should point to the 1756-DHRIO module.  In this example we have set up the DHRIO module in the I/O Configuarion.  It is named dhp and this is address which is named in the path.  This CIP Generic MSG can also be used for channel B by changing the Object ID to 2.
The tag address will show the baud rate of the DH+ node of the selected 1756-DHRIO module.  
0 = 57.6 Kbps
1 = 115.2 Kbps
2 = 230.4 Kbps

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(_Status_baud_rate.EN)MSG(_Status_baud_rate);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:3 - S:6 Active Node table for DH+ Channel 1A.
Use a CIP Generic MSG instruction to get information from the 1756-DHRIO module.   Set the values to the following:  Service Code 'e', Class Name 'a5', Instance Name '1', & Attribute Name '8'.  The Source section is left blank and the number of elements is zero.  A destination tag of the SINT type needs to be created.  In this example it is called _Status_active_tbl.  The Path should point to the 1756-DHRIO module.  In this example we have set up the DHRIO module in the I/O Configuarion.  It is named dhp and this is address which is named in the path.  
This CIP Generic MSG can also be used for channel B by changing the Object ID to 2.
The tag address should be in the SINT format and have an array of 8.  The first SINT gives the number of nodes on the link.
The second SINT will give the node addresses from 0 - 7.  If bit 2 is set than there is a device on node 2.
The third SINT will give the node addresses from 10 - 17.  If bit 4 & 6 are set than there are devices on nodes 14 & 16.
The fourth SINT will follow the same format as the second and third but for addresses 20 - 27.
This format will repeat for the fifth, sixth, seventh, & eighth SINTs.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(_Status_node_tbl.EN)MSG(_Status_node_tbl);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This MSG instruction does not have a coinciding PLC5 Status address.
Use this MSG instruction when trying to find the mode (Program, Run, Idle) of the DH+ link being analyzed.
Use a CIP Generic MSG instruction to get information from the 1756-DHRIO module.   Set the values to the following:  Service Code 'e', Class Name 'a5', Instance Name '1', & Attribute Name '9'.  The Source section is left blank and the number of elements is zero.  A destination tag of the SINT type needs to be created.  In this example it is called _Status_chn_state.  The Path should point to the 1756-DHRIO module.  In this example we have set up the DHRIO module in the I/O Configuarion.  It is named dhp and this is address which is named in the path.  
A hexidecimal value will be given to designate the following mode/state of the DH+ Link:
00    Non-existent
01  Idle
02  Wait
03  Store
04  Run
05  Stop
06 -ffff Invalid
This CIP Generic MSG can also be used for channel B by changing the Object ID to 2.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(_Status_last_state.EN)MSG(_Status_last_state);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S36_S58_PII_STI_Stuff" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:36 - S:52 are PII information which is not available
S:55 & S:56 are PII information which is not available
S:53 STI (Periodic Task) time
S:54 STI (Periodic Task) maximum scan time
S:57 User program checksum is not available
S:58 Reserved is not applicable]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:53 STI (Periodic Task) Scan Time.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the Task object and LastScanTime  Attribute to get or modify the setpoint value of the Last scan time of a particular task in microseconds.  The Instance Name can select a particular task or use the THIS name to select the current executing task.  A tag with a DINT type will need to be created for the destination.  See the GSV/SSV Task Object for specific details. ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(TASK,SecondTask,LASTSCANTIME,_Status_Task_LastScan);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:54 STI (PeriodicTask) Maximum Scan Time.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the Task object and MaxScanTime Attribute to get or modify the maximum scan time of a particular task.  The Instance Name can select a particular task or use the THIS name to select the current executing task.  A tag with a DINT type will need to be created for the destination.  See the GSV/SSV Task Object for specific details. ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(TASK,SecondTask,MAXSCANTIME,_Status_Task_MaxScan);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S59_S65_LIO_RIO_Stuff" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:59 - S:62 Is Extended Local I/O information which is not applicable
S:64 Protected processor information is not applicable
S:65 Remote Block transfer information for channel 2A/2B is not applicable
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S66_S77_TimeSlice" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:66 Reserved is not applicable
S:77 Communication Time Slice]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:77 Communication Time Slice.
Use the GSV (Get System Value) or SSV (Set System Value) commands with the Controller object and TimeSlice Attribute to get or modify the Communication Time Slice of the controller.  A tag with a INT type will need to be created for the destination.  Valid values are 10 - 90.  See the GSV/SSV Task Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(CONTROLLER,,TimeSlice,_Status_Logix_timeslice);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S78_S127_MCP_Stuff" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:78 - S:127 MCP information is not applicable]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="S8_S9_Prog_scan_times" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[S:8 Last Program Scan
S:9 Maximum Program Scan]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:8 Last Scan Time of the Program.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) command with the Program object and LastScanTime Attribute to get the value of the last scan time of the complete program.  The Instance Name can select a particular program or use the THIS name to select the current executing program.  A tag with a DINT type will need to be created for the destination.  See the GSV/SSV Program Object for specific details. ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(PROGRAM,THIS,LASTSCANTIME,_Status_Prog_Scantime);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:8  Last Scan Time of Program
Use with the GSV Program Object above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(_Status_Prog_Scantime,_Status_Program_time);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[PLC5's S:9 Maximum Scan Time of a program.   
Use the GSV (Get System Value) command with the Program object and MaxScanTime Attribute to get the value of the maximum scan time of a particular program.  The Instance Name can select a particular program or use the THIS name to select the current executing program.  A tag with a DINT type will need to be created for the destination.  See the GSV/SSV Program Object for specific details.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GSV(PROGRAM,THIS,MAXSCANTIME,_Status_max_Scantime);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
</Routines>
</Program>
